WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.197
Let us summarize the main ideas from Module 6.

00:00:03.197 --> 00:00:09.402
Start with grain. Decide what one result row should represent before writing the join.

00:00:09.402 --> 00:00:12.622
Then decide what population must be preserved.

00:00:12.622 --> 00:00:15.152
INNER JOIN keeps only matches.

00:00:15.152 --> 00:00:19.072
LEFT JOIN and RIGHT JOIN preserve one designated side.

00:00:19.072 --> 00:00:25.577
FULL OUTER JOIN preserves unmatched rows from both sides.

00:00:25.577 --> 00:00:29.992
Remember that ON controls matching, while WHERE filters the joined result.

00:00:29.992 --> 00:00:34.832
This distinction becomes especially important with outer joins.

00:00:34.832 --> 00:00:39.767
Cardinality explains why rows repeat or multiply.

00:00:39.767 --> 00:00:45.912
One-to-many repetition can be completely correct, but several one-to-many child tables joined together can

00:00:45.912 --> 00:00:48.492
create combinations that inflate counts.

00:00:48.492 --> 00:00:53.312
When your target is one row per patient, customer, student, or department,

00:00:53.312 --> 00:01:00.012
pre-aggregating child tables to the target grain is often the clearest solution.

00:01:00.012 --> 00:01:04.897
Finally, validate. Compare total rows, distinct keys, unmatched cases, and known examples.

00:01:04.897 --> 00:01:13.022
If you keep one habit from this module, use this sequence: define the grain, predict the matches,

00:01:13.022 --> 00:01:16.814
run the join, and verify the result before trusting it.
